WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.220
Temporary files are short-lived storage, but short-lived does not mean harmless.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:21.000
A temporary file may contain user data, intermediate calculations, extracted content, reports, configuration fragments, operational diagnostics, or other material that would matter if it were exposed or retained longer than expected.

3
00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:29.120
Temporary files also live in real directories with real permissions, names, owners, handles, and cleanup behavior.

4
00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:40.580
If the file is created in a shared location, named predictably, left behind after a failure, or opened through fragile path logic, it can become a security and reliability concern.

5
00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:44.220
The word temporary can create a false sense of safety.

6
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:53.200
Developers may write more data than needed, rely on a cleanup path that is rarely tested, or assume the operating system will always delete files at the right time.

7
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.020
Production systems need more deliberate rules.

8
00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.820
Safe temporary-file handling starts with design intent.

9
00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:10.400
Decide why the file is needed, what it may contain, who can access it, how it is created, when it is removed, and what happens if creation, writing, or deletion fails.

10
00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.120
Temporary files should be reviewed as real storage.

11
00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:22.540
They may be on disk, included in backups, collected by diagnostics, scanned by support tools, or left behind after a crash.

12
00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.880
That lifecycle deserves the same defensive thinking as other file-handling code.

13
00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:39.560
The goal of this course is practical: choose trusted locations, create files safely, minimize contents, control permissions, avoid race-prone path assumptions, and tie cleanup to clear ownership.

14
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:46.560
A useful review habit is to treat every temporary file as a named data asset with a lifecycle.

15
00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:54.740
If the team cannot explain where it lives, what it contains, who can read it, and when it disappears, the design needs more work.
